lis:article/10.17821%2Fsrels%2F2023%2Fv60i3%2F171028
    a lis:Article ;
    dcterms:title "Scholarly Communication and Machine-Generated Text: Is it Finally AI vs AI in Plagiarism Detection?" ;
    dcterms:issued "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    lis:citesCount 18 ;
    dcterms:description "This study utilizes GPT (Generative Pre-Trained Transformer) language model-based AI writing tools to create a set of 80 academic writing samples based on the eight themes of the experiential sessions of the LTC 2023. These samples, each between 2000 and 2500 words long, are then analyzed using both conventional plagiarism detection tools and selected AI detection tools. The study finds that traditional syntactic similarity-based anti-plagiarism tools struggle to detect AI-generated text due to the differences in syntax and structure between machine-generated and human-written text. However, t" ;
