    a lis:Article ;
    dcterms:title "Classification in the Wider Philosophical Perspective of Informational Ontology. Lecture 2: Informational Ontology" ;
    dcterms:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    lis:citesCount 2 ;
    dcterms:description "The recent informational paradigm suggests that there are relationships of formal dependence between the major levels of reality: matter, life, mind and culture, as each of them is a new way of recording models of other phenomena in some modular memory. Such memories – respectively energy, genomes, cognition and language – instruct the production of many copies of informed systems – that is bodies, organisms, behaviour and cultural heritage. The macro-level of culture is often reduced to the label of “social” but covers several sub-levels that have their characteristics. The objects of informa" ;
