    dcterms:description "A common ethical apprehension regarding artificial intelligence seems to centre around fears that it may endanger the human condition, ‘what it means to be human.’ It is thought that radical advancements in AI might irreversibly alter the experiential grounds of humanity. However, we contest that mankind, as a unified semiotic system of signs and values that anchored the Enlightenment view of the world, has been adequately critiqued and deconstructed over the past few decades. It is within this post-Enlightenment, and more importantly, post-human, context that we may engage with technological " ;
